.TH WDISPLAY 1 "October 2026" "wdisplay 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wdisplay \- monitor configuration for Window Maker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wdisplay
[\fB\-\-restore\fR] [\fB\-\-print\fR] [\fB\-\-display\fR \fIname\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wdisplay
is a WPrefs style preferences window for setting up monitors: resolution,
refresh rate, rotation, the primary display and where the displays are
placed on the desk. Drag the displays in the
.I Arrangement
box to match their physical placement and press
.BR Apply .
The new settings must be confirmed within 15 seconds or the old ones come
back.
.PP
Confirmed settings are stored per combination of connected monitors in
.I ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WDisplay
and a call to
.B wdisplay \-\-restore
is added to
.IR ~/GNUstep/Library/WindowMaker/autostart ,
so the layout is applied again at every Window Maker start.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-restore
Apply the layout saved for the monitors connected right now and exit.
Does nothing if no layout was saved for them.
.TP
.B \-\-print
Print the xrandr command that \-\-restore would run and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-display " name"
The X display to use.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WMAKER_USER_ROOT
Used instead of ~/GNUstep, as in Window Maker.
.TP
.B WDISPLAY_DRY_RUN
Print the xrandr commands instead of running them.
.TP
.B WDISPLAY_FAKE
Show a made up three monitor setup, for trying the program out.
Nothing is changed on the X server.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wmaker (1),
.BR WPrefs (1),
.BR xrandr (1)
